  "title": "Avoid quadratic name lookups when loading safetensors with CPU fallback",
  "body": "## What does this PR do?\n\nThe mixed-device safetensors loader rebuilds the flattened list of assigned weight names for every checkpoint key, then performs a linear membership search. This makes the CPU-fallback name-selection phase quadratic in the number of weights, even for a device map with only two entries.\n\nBuild the assigned-name set once, append fallback names in their original order, and release the set before materializing tensors. Existing module-prefix assignment, overlapping entries, disk-to-CPU fallback and single-device fast paths retain their behavior.\n\n## Measured loading improvement\n\n[Successful native before/after run](https://github.com/Afloat16/accelerate/actions/runs/43415172502), exact source `d31250bad01c8fb0d3b0357a976721daa5dd63dd`, upstream base `3bd795f7cfb763b994b4ba8ba59e5a305a7c528a`.\n\nFull `load_state_dict` calls on real safetensors files, with half the keys assigned to CPU and half to disk. Medians of three warm-cache loads on the same GitHub-hosted CPU runner; normal progress rendering is retained and its stderr is redirected to the null device in both measurements.\n\n| Weights | Before | After | Speedup |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 64 | 5.876 ms | 5.787 ms | 1.02x |\n| 4,096 | 242.194 ms | 177.454 ms | 1.36x |\n| 32,768 | 5.850 s | 1.485 s | 3.94x |\n\nAll output-name/value SHA-256 digests match. The 32,768-weight file is 2,811,696 bytes. This isolates name-heavy checkpoint overhead; it is not a throughput claim for large tensor payloads or GPU loading. The temporary set uses linear additional storage; measured Python peak allocation for that case is 13,015,107 versus 13,043,563 bytes. These tracemalloc numbers are not process RSS or native tensor memory.\n\n## Correctness validation\n\nPython 3.12.16, PyTorch 2.9.0+cpu, safetensors 0.8.0.\n\n- All 26 new tests pass on both unchanged upstream and the fixed source. This is a performance improvement; no numerical failure is claimed for the baseline.\n- New tests plus the entire existing `tests/test_modeling_utils.py`: **64 passed, 10 skipped, 4 subtests passed** on both revisions.\n- Cases cover mixed CPU/disk maps, missing and overlapping module prefixes, reversed map order, root and absent mappings, single-device controls, FP32/FP64 weights, boolean buffers and empty tensors.\n- Real SGD-with-momentum and AdamW checkpoints resume after two updates. Losses, parameter gradients, weights and optimizer state match uninterrupted training exactly for three further updates.\n- Project-pinned Ruff 0.13.1 lint/format and `git diff --check` pass.\n\nGPU execution, end-to-end disk-offloaded model inference and the full repository suite were not run.\n\n## Prior work checked\n\nAll-state issue/PR searches, the complete 175-commit history of the affected file, and all seven existing non-validation fork branch heads were checked. #1121 introduced the fallback expression; #1321 deduplicated the device list, #1557 corrected the single-device shortcut, #1853 changed checkpoint file discovery and #3674 optimized tensor transfers. None changes this repeated fallback membership work.\n\n## Before submitting\n\n- [x] Read the contributor guideline and searched existing work.\n- [ ] Prior issue discussion: this isolated performance change was not proposed in an issue.\n- [x] Added necessary correctness and resumed-training tests.\n- [x] No API or documented loading behavior changes; benchmark method and limits are described above.\n",
